-- title:
--   Integrality of g¹²/Δ^k over ℤ[1/M][j] and of g¹²/E₄^{3k} over ℤ[1/M][j⁻¹]
-- statement:
--   Let $M$ be a nonzero natural number and $k$ a natural number, and let $g$ be a modular form of weight $(k : \mathbb{Z})$ for the congruence subgroup $\Gamma_1(M)$, regarded as a subgroup of $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{R})$. Let $pg$ be a power series with integer coefficients such that [`ModularCurve.IsIntegralQExp g pg`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L37) holds, that is, the coefficientwise image of $pg$ in $\mathbb{C}[[q]]$ is the $q$-expansion of $g$ of width $1$. Write $R$ for the subring $\mathbb{Z}[1/M]$ of $\mathbb{Q}$ generated by $(M:\mathbb{Q})^{-1}$, and work in the field $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ of Laurent series; for an integral power series $p$, let $p_{\mathbb{Q}}$ denote its image there. Let $j$ be [`ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L157), the Laurent series $q^{-1}$ times the rational image of the numerator series of the modular invariant, $\Delta := q\prod_{n\ge 1}(1-q^{n})^{24}$ (the product `PowerSeries.X * ModularCurve.dedekindEtaUnit`) and $E_4 := 1 + 240\sum_{n\ge 1}\sigma_3(n)q^{n}$ ([`ModularCurve.eisenstein4`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L111)). The conclusion is the conjunction of two integrality statements in $\mathbb{Q}((q))$: the element $(pg^{12})_{\mathbb{Q}}/(\Delta^{k})_{\mathbb{Q}}$ is a root of a monic polynomial whose coefficients lie in the image of $R[X]$ under the evaluation $X \mapsto j$, and the element $(pg^{12})_{\mathbb{Q}}/(E_4^{3k})_{\mathbb{Q}}$ is a root of a monic polynomial whose coefficients lie in the image of $R[X]$ under the evaluation $X \mapsto j^{-1}$.
--
--   This is the chart form of the $q$-expansion principle: the weight-zero functions $g^{12}/\Delta^{k}$ and $g^{12}/E_4^{3k}$ on $\Gamma_1(M)$, read as Laurent series in $q$, are integral over $\mathbb{Z}[1/M][j]$ and over $\mathbb{Z}[1/M][j^{-1}]$ respectively, i.e. they are regular on the two standard affine charts of the $j$-line. It is used by [`ModularCurve.exists_coe_eq_div_pow_and_mul_min_ord_le_ord_x1FunctionFieldC`](thm.html#ModularCurve.exists_coe_eq_div_pow_and_mul_min_ord_le_ord_x1FunctionFieldC) in the construction of integral models and function fields of the modular curves $X_1(M)$.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_isIntegralElem_div_delta_pow_and_div_eisenstein4_pow_of_isIntegralQExp_gamma1.lean

